abstract: "North Pacific salmon (Oncorhynchus spp.) were collected during the winter\
  \ 2022 Pan-Pacific International Year of the Salmon expeditions to the North Pacific\
  \ high seas. Sampling was conducted using four vessels operating almost concomitantly\
  \ between February 21 and April 18 across the study region: the NOAA research vessel\
  \ Bell M. Shimada; R/V CCGS Sir John Franklin; R/V TINRO; and the F/V Northwest\
  \ Explorer. Trawl nets were deployed at each station in the top 50 meters of the\
  \ water column and towed for 60 minutes (night) or 90 minutes (day) at 4 to 5 knots.\
  \ A 2x2 cm sample of salmon muscle tissue was collected from above the lateral line\
  \ and in front of the dorsal fin and stored at -80\xB0C. Whole liver was collected\
  \ immediately post-mortem and stored at -20\xB0C until processing. Muscle and liver\
  \ samples were then processed in the laboratory at the University of British Columbia.\
  \ Muscle tissue was lyophilized in freeze drier for 48 hours, whereas liver samples\
  \ were oven-dried at 50\xB0C for the same duration. Dried tissue was homogenized\
  \ using mortar and pestle, encapsulated in tin (Sn) capsules, and sent for measurements\
  \ of carbon and nitrogen stable isotope ratios and elemental contents at an external\
  \ lab. Samples were analysed on an Elemental Analyser coupled to an Isotope Ratio\
  \ Mass Spectrometer. The data reported includes each sample \u1E9F13C, %C, \u1E9F\
  15N, % N, and CN ratios, in addition to the metadata associated with each sample\
  \ (e.g., coordinates of oceanographic station and bottom depth where salmon specimen\
  \ was collected, etc.)."
